3. How to Install and Run
-------------------------
No installation step is required: the project is plain source code that
is compiled directly.

C++ version (Linux, WSL, or any POSIX system):
    cd <the folder containing this project>
    g++ main.cpp Semaphore.cpp -o dinPhil -std=c++11 -pthread
    ./dinPhil
    (stop with Ctrl+C)

  On Windows the same command works in PowerShell as long as g++ is
  installed, because the code no longer depends on <unistd.h>.

Go version:
    cd <the folder containing this project>
    go run dinPhil.go
    (stop with Ctrl+C)


4. Required Libraries and Platform Issues
-----------------------------------------
C++: C++11 or later, plus the pthread library (-pthread).
     Developed and tested on Ubuntu 24.04 running under WSL2 on Windows 11
     with g++ 15. No third-party libraries are used.

Go:  Go 1.22 or later. The code uses math/rand/v2 and the new
     "range over an integer" form, both of which require Go 1.22+.

Platform issues encountered and resolved:
  a) std::vector<Semaphore> does not compile. Semaphore contains a
     std::mutex and a std::condition_variable, which are non-copyable and
     non-movable. std::vector requires a move constructor for reallocation
     even when reserve() is called, so the container was changed to
     std::deque, which never relocates existing elements.

  b) Console output interleaved between threads (for example
     "43 is chomping!" instead of two separate lines). A chain of <<
     operations on std::cout is not atomic. A dedicated std::mutex
     (print_mutex) now protects each print statement.

  c) The original code used <unistd.h> and sleep(), which are POSIX only.
     Replaced with std::this_thread::sleep_for so the C++ version builds
     unchanged on Windows, Linux and macOS.


5. TO DO / Known Issues
-----------------------
Completed from the original template's MISSING list:
  [x] README written
  [x] Full licence information added (file headers + LICENSE file)
  [x] Comments added to all functions
  [x] Deadlock removed (odd-even fork acquisition strategy)

Remaining limitations (not required by the lab):
  - Both versions run forever by design; they are stopped with Ctrl+C.
  - No controlled shutdown / signal handling is implemented.
  - The number of philosophers is a compile-time constant (5) and the
    fork index geometry (index, index+1) assumes a ring of 5.
  - The C++ version has not been extended to log timestamps or to
    produce statistics; the console output is the only evidence.
  - In the Go version the random duration is rand.IntN(5), which can
    return 0, so a philosopher may occasionally think or eat for zero
    seconds and appear to act twice in a row. This comes from the original
    template and does not affect correctness.


6. List of Files and What They Contain
--------------------------------------
LICENSE          - GNU GPL v3 licence terms for this project.
README           - This file.
.gitignore       - Excludes compiled binaries from version control.
Semaphore.h      - Declaration of the Semaphore class. Contains the
                   templated Wait(timeout) member function, which has to be
                   defined in the header because C++ template member
                   functions cannot be compiled from a .cpp file.
Semaphore.cpp    - Implementation of Semaphore::Wait() (P operation) and
                   Semaphore::Signal() (V operation), built on std::mutex
                   and std::condition_variable.
main.cpp         - The C++ Dining Philosophers program. Creates five
                   philosopher threads, gives each fork a binary semaphore
                   initialised to 1, and uses the odd-even strategy to
                   acquire forks.
dinPhil.go       - The Go Dining Philosophers program. Each fork is a
                   buffered channel of capacity 1, used as a binary
                   semaphore; the same odd-even strategy prevents deadlock.


7. Any Other Relevant Information
---------------------------------
The problem
  Five philosophers sit around a circular table with five forks. Each
  philosopher alternates between thinking and eating, and needs both the
  fork to their left and the fork to their right in order to eat.

The naive solution and its failure
  If every philosopher picks up the left fork first and then the right
  fork, all five can hold one fork at the same time and then wait forever
  for the second one. This is a deadlock: the four Coffman conditions
  (mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait) are all
  satisfied.

The solution used here (odd-even strategy)
  Odd-numbered philosophers take the left fork first, even-numbered
  philosophers take the right fork first. This removes the circular wait
  condition, so deadlock is impossible while mutual exclusion is still
  guaranteed.

Observed behaviour
  Both programs run indefinitely with all five philosophers cycling
  through think -> eat -> think. No deadlock occurs and no philosopher
  starves. Output of the C++ version was inspected line by line: no two
  philosophers ever hold the same fork at the same time.

Alternative solutions that could be used
  - Limit the number of philosophers allowed to pick up forks at once to
    four, so at least one can always get both.
  - Use a timeout on the second Wait() and release the first fork on
    failure (already supported by Semaphore::Wait(duration)).
  - Introduce a single arbitrator semaphore around the whole table
    (correct but reduces concurrency to one eater at a time).
